::STAT_U02_Pretest_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U02_Pretest_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
In item 1, Two classes have quiz-score distributions with similar medians but different spreads. Which response best applies measures of center, measures of variability, distribution shape, outliers, and normal models to support a statistically valid conclusion for the lesson topic: 
{
~A. Select the graph or rule that looks most familiar without checking the variables. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~B. Use only the largest number in the data set as the conclusion. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~C. Assume the result proves causation because two variables appear related. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
=D. Compare both center and variability before deciding how the data sets differ. #Correct. This answer uses the lesson concept in context, connects the data or model to the question, and supports a valid statistical conclusion.
}

::STAT_U02_Pretest_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U02_Pretest_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.5
Question:
For item 2, A data set has one unusually high value compared with the rest of the observations. What should a student do first to analyze the situation using measures of center, measures of variability, distribution shape, outliers, and normal models in the context of 
{
=A. Consider whether an outlier changes the mean more than the median. #Correct. This answer uses the lesson concept in context, connects the data or model to the question, and supports a valid statistical conclusion.
~B. Use only the largest number in the data set as the conclusion. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~C. Assume the result proves causation because two variables appear related. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~D. Ignore the context and report only a formula or isolated calculation. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
}

::STAT_U02_Pretest_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U02_Pretest_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.6
Question:
Item 3 asks about Unit 2 Synthesis. A coach compares practice times using mean, median, IQR, and standard deviation. Which interpretation is most appropriate when using 
{
~A. Assume the result proves causation because two variables appear related. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
=B. Use IQR or standard deviation to describe spread based on the situation. #Correct. This answer uses the lesson concept in context, connects the data or model to the question, and supports a valid statistical conclusion.
~C. Ignore the context and report only a formula or isolated calculation. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~D. Use the same method for every problem without checking conditions. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
}

